# Title: Potential Registry Persistence Attempt Via Windows Telemetry
# ID: 73a883d0-0348-4be4-a8d8-51031c2564f8
# Status: test
# Level: high
# Author: Lednyov Alexey, oscd.community, Sreeman
# Date: 2020-10-16
# Tags: attack.privilege-escalation, attack.execution, attack.persistence, attack.t1053.005
# Description: Detects potential persistence behavior using the windows telemetry registry key.
# Windows telemetry makes use of the binary CompatTelRunner.exe to run a variety of commands and perform the actual telemetry collections.
# This binary was created to be easily extensible, and to that end, it relies on the registry to instruct on which commands to run.
# The problem is, it will run any arbitrary command without restriction of location or type.
# Converted by: Sigma Universal SIEM/EDR CLI

((registry.path:*\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\AppCompatFlags\\TelemetryController\\* AND registry.path:*\\Command AND (registry.data.strings:*.bat* OR registry.data.strings:*.bin* OR registry.data.strings:*.cmd* OR registry.data.strings:*.dat* OR registry.data.strings:*.dll* OR registry.data.strings:*.exe* OR registry.data.strings:*.hta* OR registry.data.strings:*.jar* OR registry.data.strings:*.js* OR registry.data.strings:*.msi* OR registry.data.strings:*.ps* OR registry.data.strings:*.sh* OR registry.data.strings:*.vb*)) AND NOT (((registry.data.strings:*\\system32\\CompatTelRunner.exe* OR registry.data.strings:*\\system32\\DeviceCensus.exe*))))
